\title{lifr: import, plot, and report LIF borehole logs}
\description{
Laser-Induced Fluorescence (LIF) probes such as UVOST and TarGOST are
pushed into the ground and record a fluorescence response at a fixed depth
interval. Each boring produces one log file. \code{lifr} reads those logs into a
tidy data frame, joins boring coordinates from a locations file, lets you
mask known-bad depth intervals with a recorded audit trail, summarises and
plots the data, and renders a site report.
}
\section{Data model}{

Every importer returns a \link{lif_data} object: a data frame with one row per
depth reading and at least the columns \code{boring}, \code{depth} (ft below ground
surface, downward-positive), and \code{signal} (LIF response, \%RE). When a
locations file is joined the frame also carries \code{easting}, \code{northing},
and \code{msl} (ground-surface elevation, ft). Optional instrument channels are
canonicalised to \code{ec} (electrical conductivity, mS/m), \code{hp} (hydraulic
push pressure, psi), and \code{color} (emission-wavelength hex colour). Every
other column in the source file is kept under a cleaned snake_case name,
so nothing recorded by the instrument is lost at import.
}

\section{\%RE is a screening proxy}{

LIF response is relative fluorescence, expressed as a percentage of a
reference emitter (\%RE). It indicates where fluorescent product is likely
present. It is not a concentration and is not comparable to regulatory
criteria; \code{lifr} never maps \%RE onto such criteria.
}

